        "before": "Definition 3.2, p.\u00a011, defines rescaled shading as the union of \\((\\delta/\\rho)\\)-cubes meeting the affine image of the shading. In Lemma 3.3, pp.\u00a012\u201313, equation (3.5) asserts, for \\(n=3\\), \\[\\left|\\bigcup_{T\\in\\mathbb T}Y(T)\\right|\n \\sim \\rho^2\n \\left|\\bigcup_{\\widehat T\\in\\widehat\\mathbb T}\n              \\widehat Y(\\widehat T)\\right|.\n \\tag{F1.1}\\] Its justification (3.4) is a Jacobian identity for raw images, not for their cubical enlargement. Relevant TeX lines are 420\u2013425, 456\u2013494, and 516\u2013526. The final average-multiplicity identity on p.\u00a013 also needs a separate justification after enlargement.",
        "after": "Definition 3.2, p.\u00a011, defines rescaled shading as the union of \\((\\delta/\\rho)\\)-cubes meeting the affine image of the shading. In Lemma 3.3, pp.\u00a012\u201313, equation (3.5) asserts, for \\(n=3\\), \\[\\left|\\bigcup_{T\\in\\mathbb T}Y(T)\\right|\n \\sim \\rho^2\n \\left|\\bigcup_{\\widehat T\\in\\widehat{\\mathbb T}}\n              \\widehat Y(\\widehat T)\\right|.\n \\tag{F1.1}\\] Its justification (3.4) is a Jacobian identity for raw images, not for their cubical enlargement. Relevant TeX lines are 420\u2013425, 456\u2013494, and 516\u2013526. The final average-multiplicity identity on p.\u00a013 also needs a separate justification after enlargement.",
